A||||11||fiero56||Let it warm up?||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||02-01-2001||12:26 PM||||Something I have always thought about, but never really heard anything on it.<P>Is it better for an engine to warm up for a few minutes before driving off, or is it fine to just start up and go? I try to let it run for a minute before driving off first thing in the morning. But there are times I sleep in, and need to be on the road fast, so I just start the engine and am off down the road. The highway is only a mile away too, so in 2 minutes I'm cruising at 70mph. I always seem to think about the rings loosing their seating when pushed very hard when cold, but I've never heard anything good or bad.<BR>Most new car manuals these days say don't let the engine warm up before driving. Is that just for emmisions, or is it better not to allow an engine to warm up for a minute?||||reg||5
Z||000001||loafer87gt||02-01-2001||12:35 PM||||I usually plug my car in at night, and let it run for a few minutes before taking off. I think what they reccomend against doing is letting the car sit outside running for 1/2 hour to warm up before you drive anywhere.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-01-2001||12:59 PM||||Technically you should let the car warm-up about a minute when it's cold outside. That way the cylinder temps can burn off the acids. The condensation that happens in the cylinders contains acids. This is a by-product of any combustion engine. Taking off too fast can cause the cylinder to heat up too fast and cause more condensation. Although it is a small amount and doing it "when your running late" is probably fine.<BR>There is a website that goes into detail about oil and cold start-ups. If I find it I'll post it.<P><BR>Here it is: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/chrislonghurst/engineoil_bible.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/chrislonghurst/engineoil_bible.html</A>  <BR>Here is the quote:<BR><B>"Fact is, large quantities of water are produced by the normal combustion process and, depending on engine wear, some of it gets into the crank case. If you have a good crank case breathing system it gets removed from there PDQ, but even so, in cold weather a lot of condensation will take place. This is bad enough in itself, since water is not noted for its lubrication qualities in an engine, but even worse, that water disolves any nitrates formed during the combustion process. If my memory of chemistry serves me right, that leaves you with a mixture of Nitric (HNO3) and Nitrous (HNO2) acid circulating round your engine! So not only do you suffer a high rate of wear at start-up and when the engine is cold, you suffer a high rate of subsequent corrosion </B><P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||02-01-2001||03:17 PM||||I wonder if the cold temps are why my Formula seems to have tons of water under the oil cap. I think I'll replace the PCV valve next time I'm at the parts store.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||02-01-2001||03:29 PM||||I always do a short warm up before taking off. <P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000005||overlord||02-01-2001||08:04 PM||||if it is below -15C i will have it plugged in for at least 4 hours, then after starting it, let it run for 15 minutes, and then i drive at 70 kph for about 2 miles before going a hundred kph or higher.  I always heard that an eninge shouldnt need to run for more than a coupple minutes, anythi;ng more and u are just wasting gas. But i hate driving for those first few miles with a freezing interior.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||02-01-2001||11:11 PM||||Now that it's winter and cold I usually always warm my car up at least 15 minutes.  Gives me some peace of mind and I don't have to freeze my cajones off.. In the summer I try to let it idle for a minute or so.<P>I haven't found any condensation under my cap.  Replaced the PCV valve last summer.||||reg||
Z||000007||speed racer||02-01-2001||11:47 PM||||I usably let mine run till it hits 100 degrees because if I don’t the car will all most die when I let off the clutch starting out.||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||02-02-2001||12:31 AM||||i don't let mine warm up because it will overheat. the shorter the time the car is on, the better. for me anyway. #$%&@!!!!!<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||02-02-2001||08:22 AM||||I always let mine warm up for no more then 3 min.<P>If your car is overheating, you have another problem. Perhaps your fan is not comming on, or the thermostat is stuck? Might want to check those out, I leaned the hard way overheadting sucks!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||02-02-2001||11:00 PM||||I let mine warm up long enough for me to light a cigarette and start a CD (can't ride to work without my tunes  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).  By the time I fumble for my lighter and drop the CD 2 or 3 times, I've successfully wasted at least 3 or 4 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sleeper||02-03-2001||10:15 PM||||i've found out that if i let my fiero warm up in the morning it drives better through-out the day, though it may be my imagination. <P>------------------<BR>17" Racing Hart C5 wheels, Kumho Ecsta Supra 712 tires, Intrax lowering spring, KYB shocks, KYB-GR2 struts and Chris West's Strut Tower Bar.||||reg||1
